== Avi Widgerson

=== Chapter 4 (continued)

Most problems are phrased as $x in L$ (string $x$ is in a language as covered in 240.) and a question with multiple outputs simply computes "is the first bit of output n" multiple times to get the full result. Reminds me of how $RR^n -> RR^m$ maps can be safely split into $m$ seperate $RR^n -> R$ functions.

Chapter 4 covers CSPs and Unique Games Conjecture (which was recently proven true by OpenAI).

Every csp problem has a hard proportion $p$ such that solving less than or equal to $p$ is $P$. And above $p$ is $N P$-hard.

Unique Games. The simple phrasing is in terms of linear equations. But an interesting concept is _interactive proof systems_ where an unlimited computational capacity prover is trying to convince a limited computational power verifier that a statement is true.

This is similar to NP class where task is to produce a polynomial size proof. These prover-verifier setups get more interesting if you have 2 provers, and a verifier with access to randomness. This turns the setup into a sort of zero-knowledge proof game type thing. (hence unique games name).

Next Avi covers distP which is average case running time on distributions. Apparently randomness screws with many things, especially with weird distributions. The solution is a "efficiently samplable distribution" which is a distribution that can be efficiently generated by an algorithm with access to a coin.

This is a rather creative way to define things, and I will keep this idea of "nice inputs are those which can be easily created on purpose" it sounds like it would be useful.

Another interesting assumption is "nature does not perform inefficient computations" which is used to restrict the sorts of problems that arise in practice.

The chapter finishes with a primer on cryptography, most of which I already know to some extent. (one way hash functions, modular arithmetic for diffe-hellman).

Noteable functions are that factoring integers is hard, and that taking logarithm in modular arithmetic is hard.

